Rindler coordinates(1) are coordinates appropriate for an observer undergoing constant proper acceleration (a constant g-force felt) in an otherwise flat spacetime. Given an unprimed inertial frame set of coordinates, one assigns the accelerated frame observer primed coordinates, "Rindler coordinates", given by

:   {\displaystyle ct=\left({\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}+x'\right)\sinh \left({\frac {\alpha t'}{c}}\right)}

:   {\displaystyle x=\left({\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}+x'\right)\cosh \left({\frac {\alpha t'}{c}}\right)-{\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}}

where {\displaystyle \alpha } is the constant proper acceleration.

## Developement

[edit | edit source] [...] We merely make a natural choice of primed coordinates for which {\displaystyle x'=0} is described by those equations of motion. Rindler's choice was

:   {\displaystyle ct=\left({\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}+x'\right)\sinh \left({\frac {\alpha t'}{c}}\right)}

:   {\displaystyle x=\left({\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}+x'\right)\cosh \left({\frac {\alpha t'}{c}}\right)-{\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}}

which does just that.

## Rindler Metric

[edit | edit source]

Rindler's spacetime is then just an accelerated frame transformation of the Minkowski metric of special relativity. Doing the transformation

:   {\displaystyle ct=\left({\frac {c^{2}}{\alpha }}+x'\right)\sinh \left({\frac {\alpha t'}{c}}\right)} [...] :   {\displaystyle ct=\int \gamma dct'+\gamma \beta x'}

:   {\displaystyle x=\gamma x'+\int \gamma \beta dct'}

:   {\displaystyle y=y'}

:   {\displaystyle z=z'}

where we are doing anti-derivatives with respect to the primed coordinate time.

It turns out that Rindler's choice of coordinates for an observer undergoing constant proper acceleration is the special case of this transformation where {\displaystyle \beta } and {\displaystyle \gamma } are what you would get as a function of the primed time if they were describing constant proper acceleration. Doing this more general transformation for arbitrary time dependent acceleration the line element transforms to

:   {\displaystyle ds^{2}=\left(1+{\frac {\alpha x'}{c^{2}}}\right)^{2}dct'^{2}-\left(dx'^{2}+dy'^{2}+dz'^{2}\right)}
